{
  "name": "Exercise can help reduce the risk of Parkinson's disease: experts",
  "parent_id": 203313630673674,
  "group_id": "195c5757-c562-4672-91ad-966589408132",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-30T14:41:30.300Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-07-30T14:41:30.432Z",
  "published_at": "2026-07-30T14:41:30.416Z",
  "id": 203705918742819,
  "uuid": "933e6a9a-3d7d-4b5c-8a86-952e770cce5e",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "07606348-2f84-a3fe-4fa0-cc41f1c88a60",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "4e67864a-8561-f97a-04a4-85122eb11b16",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Medical practitioners referred to recent studies in Sweden and Australia that suggest that people can lower their risk of getting Parkinson's disease by incorporating simple exercises in their daily routine. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Increasing exposure to chemicals in daily life have shown to increase the incidence of Pakinson's in developed countries. \"Besides household chemicals, government has to carefully reassess our agriculture polices about use of pesticides on edible crops,\" said Dr Mughis Sheerani, Consultant Neurologist, Aga Khan University Hospital. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"Pesticides can be found on food even after washing and has long-term effects on human health,\" he added.   ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Parkinson's affects the person with the disease and their family. Experts highlighted the problems that people living with Parkinson's face in their everyday lives and ways to conquer them. It is important to remain positive and maintain a healthy and active lifestyle. A variety of medications are available to effectively treat the symptoms and help people lead a full life.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "\"Engaging in social activities, hobbies, regular physiotherapy, and religious or spiritual activities are helpful coping mechanisms,\" said Dr Qurat Khan, Consultant Psychiatrist, AKUH. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Parkinson's patients need to maintain a healthy lifestyle and learn to identify stress triggers, she added. Family and friends should also spend quality time with patients.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Doctors discussed various treatments for Parkinson's disease as well. They explained that medicines are the main tools to manage the disease, since surgical options are limited and reserved for a select group of patients. Deep Brain Stimulation is a surgical procedure used for treating specific symptoms of Parkinson's disease. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The University Hospital has been running a comprehensive Parkinson's rehabilitation program for several years, to help people living with Parkinson's manage the disease.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Other speakers at the occasion included Dr Shahzad Shamim, Dr Qurat Khan, Mr Mohabat Ali and Mr Haroon Bashir.\"",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
